## 6. INVENTORY MANAGEMENT

Inventory must represent the product quantities associated with a particular business.

Maintain the appropriate product and inventory relationships, including:

* Business ID.
* Product ID or internal product reference.
* SKU, where applicable.
* Product name.
* Brand.
* Opening stock.
* Approved purchase movements.
* Approved sales movements.
* Returns and stock adjustments, when supported.
* Current stock.
* Reserved or committed quantity, if supported.
* Available stock.
* Disputed or quarantined quantity, if applicable.
* Last updated timestamp.

Do not treat SKU, product ID, brand, and product name as interchangeable fields.

Use a consistent product identity model to match products across invoices and businesses.

### Stock calculation

Where all movements are represented correctly:

Current Stock = Opening Stock + Approved Stock Increases - Approved Stock Decreases + Approved Adjustments

Available Stock must account for any stock already committed to other transactions.

Do not count an invoice as a completed stock movement merely because someone uploaded it.

An invoice that is pending verification must not automatically increase available stock.

Stock must only change according to explicit, authorized, auditable inventory events.

Prevent the same transaction from affecting stock more than once.

Use atomic database operations, unique transaction references, idempotency controls, or equivalent mechanisms appropriate to the existing architecture.

Never allow a rejected invoice or an unapproved transaction to create an ordinary approved stock increase.

