## 10. CRITICAL RULE: MULTI-SOURCE STOCK RECONCILIATION

A business must be able to purchase the same product from multiple legitimate suppliers.

Do not permanently bind a business to a single upstream source.

Consider the following example.

### Initial stock

Wholesaler B previously received 1,000 units from Distributor A.

Its records show:

* Approved purchase from Distributor A: 1,000 units.
* Approved sales to other buyers: 700 units.
* Remaining stock: 300 units.

Wholesaler B now receives a requirement to supply 500 units to Supplier C.

Its currently available stock is only 300 units.

The system must not automatically approve a 500-unit stock movement simply because an invoice was submitted.

It must identify the 200-unit shortfall.

### Alternative source

Wholesaler B subsequently purchases 200 additional units from Distributor B.

Assume that the new purchase passes the applicable verification rules and its stock movement is properly supported.

The stock calculation becomes:

Previous available stock: 300 units.

Additional approved stock: 200 units.

Total available stock: 500 units.

Proposed supply to Supplier C: 500 units.

Remaining available stock after approval: 0 units.

The system may approve the 500-unit supply transaction if all applicable transaction, inventory, and evidence requirements are satisfied.

The 200-unit purchase from Distributor B must have its own traceable transaction record.

The system must not fabricate stock, ignore existing reservations, or count the same stock twice.

If physical batch-level traceability is unavailable, use transparent quantity-based reconciliation and identify its limitations.

Do not claim that a particular unit physically came from a specific batch unless the evidence supports that conclusion.

