## 19. REQUIRED TEST SCENARIOS

Before declaring the implementation complete, verify these cases using automated tests where the existing project supports them.

TEST 1: Valid transaction.

A supported invoice and sufficient approved stock result in the expected decision and inventory movement.

TEST 2: Insufficient stock.

A proposed sale exceeds available stock. The system must not approve the unsupported quantity.

TEST 3: Multiple sources.

Wholesaler B has 300 available units and receives an independently supported additional 200 units from Distributor B. A 500-unit sale can be approved if every applicable rule passes.

TEST 4: Duplicate processing.

The same approved transaction is processed twice. Inventory must only change once.

TEST 5: Duplicate invoice.

The same invoice is submitted again. The system detects the potential duplicate and prevents duplicate stock movement.

TEST 6: Unrelated upstream issue.

Distributor A has a discrepancy in an unrelated transaction. A properly supported transaction from Distributor B remains unaffected.

TEST 7: Directly connected upstream issue.

A transaction depends on disputed stock. The system identifies the dependency and applies the appropriate review or rejection policy.

TEST 8: Mixed-source quantity.

Only part of a proposed transaction is supported by undisputed stock. The system identifies the supported and disputed quantities without silently changing the invoice.

TEST 9: Later evidence.

A previously approved upstream transaction is later materially disputed. The system preserves the original decision, identifies affected transactions, and creates reassessment events.

TEST 10: Privacy.

An unauthorized business cannot access confidential records belonging to another business.

TEST 11: Missing evidence.

The system does not claim physical delivery, business legitimacy, or product authenticity without sufficient supporting evidence.

TEST 12: Failed transaction.

An interrupted approval does not leave stock movements duplicated or inconsistent.

TEST 13: Multi-line invoice.

A single invoice containing multiple products updates the appropriate inventory records without mixing product quantities.

TEST 14: Corresponding invoices.

The system can link matching issued and received invoice records without assuming that similar-looking references always represent the same transaction.

TEST 15: Audit history.

A subsequent status change does not silently erase the original decision or its supporting evidence.

Report any test that cannot be performed because the current application lacks the required backend, data, or integration.

